  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/016286e82482af83783f0eef6361591861b564643d4d9f010f2b82999fcaad27.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$ Hence, $c=-4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}-4 & =-2 \\\\ k-4 & =-2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-(-4)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4.$",
